The `pop()` method removes and returns the element at the front of the queue, also rebalancing the tree if necessary.

The `front()` method simply returns the value at the front of the queue without removing it.

The `back()` method returns the value at the back of the queue without removing it.

The `see_saw()` method calculates the sum of adjacent elements (i.e., pairs `(Qi, Qi+1)`) and returns the result in O(n) time.

The `_isbalanced()`, `_height()`, `_siftdown()`, `_siftup()`, and `_compare()` methods are helper functions for balancing the tree and comparing elements.

Note that the space complexity of this data structure is O(n) since we store pairs of integers for each element in the queue, thus introducing an additional O(n) factor. However, all operations (insert, remove, check empty, front, back, and see_saw()) run in O(log n) or O(n) time, ensuring constant amortized time complexity.

